.\" Manual page for the principals map pam_ssoossh reads.
.TH PAM_SSOOSSH\-PRINCIPALS 5 "2026-09-05" "pam_ssoossh" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
pam_ssoossh\-principals \- which certificate principals may assume which local account
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file named by
.BR pam_ssoossh (8)'s
.B principals\-map
argument maps each local account to the certificate principals allowed to
authenticate as it. It is consulted by the third of the module's four
checks.
.PP
A certificate's principals are set by
.B ssoosshd
and, for a PAM request, carry the identity of the person who
.I approved
it \(em their username as the identity provider spells it, and any further
accounts the server records them as holding \(em never the local account
that was asked for. So a host whose local accounts are not named exactly
like those identities needs this file for anyone to log in at all, and a
host that wants
.I alice
to be able to become
.I deploy
needs it too. The module treats the file as optional; a deployment rarely
can.
.SH FORMAT
A fixed subset of YAML, read without a YAML library. What is accepted:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# who may become each account
alice:              # a local account, at the start of the line
  \- alice           # a principal allowed to assume it
  \- admin           # indentation is free; "\- " marks an item
bob: [bob, ops]     # a flow sequence on the account's own line
carol:              # no principals: nobody may become carol
dave: ~             # null and ~ mean the same as nothing
.fi
.RE
.PP
Comments start with
.B #
and run to the end of the line. Account names and principals may be wrapped
in matching single or double quotes, which are stripped. Escapes are not
interpreted: a quoted value containing a backslash, a quote of the same
kind, or \(em inside a flow sequence \(em a comma, is rejected rather than
guessed at. Anything else YAML allows \(em nested mappings, anchors,
multi\-line scalars, multiple documents, a key appearing twice \(em is a
parse error.
.PP
Matching is exact and case\-sensitive on both sides.
.SH SEMANTICS
.TP
The map loads.
It is authoritative for every account on the host. A certificate passes
check 3 when one of its principals is listed under the account being
authenticated. An account with no entry is denied, even when a certificate
principal happens to equal its name: absence from the file is a decision,
not an oversight.
.TP
No map is configured.
The certificate must carry the local account name itself as a principal.
.TP
The map is configured but cannot be loaded.
Missing, unreadable, or malformed: the module logs a warning saying so and
behaves as if no map were configured. The login is not failed, so a
mistyped path or a bad edit degrades to the
.I stricter
policy rather than locking every account out of the host. That is also why
the parser is strict: a file that parses one way here and another way
elsewhere would change policy silently.
.SH LIMITS
Up to 64 principals per account, each up to 127 bytes. Only the entry for
the account being authenticated is kept, but the whole file is parsed and
validated on every attempt, because a malformed line anywhere decides
whether the map loads at all.
.SH EXAMPLE
A host where the identity provider's usernames are e\-mail local parts and
two people share an operations account:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# /etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml
alice:  [alice]
bob:    [bob]
deploy: [alice, bob]
root:              # nobody becomes root through ssoossh
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.I /etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml
is the path the shipped examples use. Nothing in the module fixes it. Owned
by root, readable by the users of the services that load the module.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_ssoossh (8),
.BR pam_ssoossh\-ca (5)
